implementationPlan.description:
THE RULE, chosen from the two the task offers: Moe's delivery records hold SHA-1 object ids only. A repository whose object format is sha256 is refused up front and lands nothing, exactly like a no-git project.

Why refuse rather than accept 64-hex: accepting means changing the daemon regexes and every '7-40 hex' / '40 hex' message, tool description and doc line, plus the wrappers' zero-oid and empty-tree constants, their replay regexes and the harness fakes. That is a lot of surface for a rare format, against the epic's 'one narrow vertical slice' rail. Supporting sha256 later is a separate feature.

RED FIRST, in BOTH harnesses (identical frozen-suite JS). This task waits on task-d1441364, task-84150869 and task-a5fb02e9, which edit these files, so find code by name.
1. Add frozen mode 'sha256-repo'. Its fixture uses git(repo,'init','-q','--object-format=sha256') instead of the plain init; everything else stays as in 'pass'. Skip the mode, printing why, if `git init --object-format=sha256` is unsupported, which needs git older than 2.29 (CI and this box have newer).
2. Assertions:
   (a) the log contains 'MOE_COMMIT_REFUSED_OBJECT_FORMAT' and 'sha256';
   (b) HEAD is unchanged and no refs/moe/rescue/* exist;
   (c) no record_candidate, record_check_run or gate observation, since nothing is frozen or gated;
   (d) exactly one finalize, outcome 'nothing-to-commit' (the documented no-git acknowledgement);
   (e) owned.txt still holds the CLI's bytes, uncommitted.
3. It must FAIL on today's wrappers. Their landing freezes a candidate with a 64-hex tree, the fake refuses it (it checks 40 hex, like the daemon), and the bytes are rescued. So (a), (b) and (d) are red. Run it on sh and pwsh and paste the failures.
